# The upstream serves a CORRECT, highly COMPRESSIBLE reply and DRIPS it slowly
# enough to still be in flight when the reload lands. Two properties matter here
# that plain hup-storm-mid-transfer does not care about:
#
#  * COMPRESSIBLE: the value is a long run of repeated blocks, so zstd shrinks it
#    to a small fraction of its input. The driver asserts the wire body is
#    STRICTLY SMALLER than the plaintext -- proof the response was actually
#    compressed, not passed through, so "the CCtx survived the reload" is a claim
#    about a real compression context and not a no-op.
#  * DRIPPED across the reload: 2 bytes / 250 ms keeps the memcached fetch (and
#    therefore the zstd body filter feeding on it) alive for several seconds --
#    several times the cost of the one or two HUPs the driver fires, each of
#    which is absorbed in well under a second. So on any non-pathological box the
#    compression context is genuinely mid-stream at every HUP; if a pathological
#    host ever reloaded slower than the drip finished, the driver's inflight-alive
#    gate reds (held request ended too early) rather than passing falsely -- the
#    conservative direction, a false RED that says "re-tune the drip", never a
#    false GREEN.
#
# The value length is deliberately a clean multiple of the 64-byte block so the
# plaintext checksum the driver compares against is trivial to regenerate: it is
# exactly the same bytes seeded here.
#
# Gated by backend-smoke like every other fake upstream: an adversarially-timed
# reply proves nothing unless the same fake is first proven correct told to
# behave normally.
proto   memcached
seed    slow    AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAABBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDD

# on=get:1 so ONLY the first (held) request drips; the post-reload coherence leg
# (get:2, ...) is served at full speed and is not itself what spans the reload.
fault   on=get:1    action=drip    bytes=2    ms=250
